al` | Packaged command supports dataset selection, run count, isolated home, and structured score output. | The c198a33f archive was installed through the production installer and its package-runtime acceptance passed 5/5 in an uncontended rerun. | **PACKAGE RUNTIME PASS — c198a33f RC2** | `eval/package_runtime.test.mjs` log SHA-256 `045f31fa6c0d85f132c37d49134f62e03c61ad839fdaab52c1da2b42ffba94d1`; the test used isolated HOME and the exact archive. Repeat against the final stable archive. |\n | WP-1105 — Deterministic code evaluators | Modular AST, build, typecheck, diff-boundary, and JSON-repair evaluators. | Integrated in commit `e2bacf1a`; codex-root fixed review findings through `2dbe565b` (symlink overlay, Rust diagnostics, coded-diagnostic pattern). | **SOURCE GATES PASS; INDEPENDENT REVIEW REQUEST CHANGES, FINDINGS WAIVED** | `code_evaluators.test.ts` passes on the final head (see the final gate). Open items are listed in [the release notes](v1.1.2.md#code-evaluators-wp-1105): fail-open overlay for non-listed file types, shadowing through `node_modules` and build helpers, snapshot limits, no resource limits. The evaluators are not used by any dataset task yet. |\n-| WP-1106 — Single-step loop mode | `--step` and `/step` pause deterministically after one agent turn for operator confirmation. | Integrated in commits `1cc28f94` and `faca6e6c`. CLI flag `--step`, REPL `/step`, and TUI `/step` catalogue entry with session command handler. | **PASSED** | Validated in `cli/test/step_loop.test.ts` (8/8 passed) and `cli/test/slash_router.test.ts` (34/34 passed). |\n+| WP-1106 — Single-step loop mode | `--step` and `/step` pause deterministically after one agent turn for operator confirmation. | Integrated in commits `1cc28f94` and `faca6e6c`. CLI flag `--step`, REPL `/step`, and TUI `/step` catalogue entry with session command handler. Entire's automated review of PR #402 found a HIGH defect (the parallel `read_file` path skipped the pause); it was reproduced and fixed in `822c08fb` with a regression test (`AC1b`) that failed first. | **SOURCE PASSES; ONE HIGH FIXED; MEDIUM FINDINGS OPEN (TUI /step) — OWNER-ACCEPTED LIMITATIONS** | `step_loop.test.ts` passes 9/9 on the fixed head. The REPL `/step` test does not exercise the real command, and the TUI `/step` handler has no test. Open items are listed in [the release notes](v1.1.2.md#single-step-mode-and-the-tui-wp-1106). |\n | WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Commit `1518780d` adds the bounded schema compiler. Commits through `0d506762` connect strict function schemas on the **vLLM request path only**; llama.cpp and Ollama requests keep their native tool schemas (no grammar or format constraint), and the hybrid reasoning grammars in the roadmap are not im…394 tokens truncated…lel path now pauses exactly like the sequential path. A regression test (`AC1b` in `cli/test/step_loop.test.ts`) was written first and failed before the fix. This was found by Entire's automated review of PR #402 and reproduced.\n+\n ### A crash-resumed unit no longer receives a multi-message prompt's later messages twice\n \n When a work unit's prompt carried two or more operator or inter-unit messages, and Nexus stopped after submitting that prompt but before the unit's result was saved, resume recognised only the **first** message as already submitted. Every later message from that prompt was injected into the unit's conversation again.\n@@ -59,6 +63,27 @@ Independent reviewers examined the release source and returned REQUEST CHANGES o\n - **Build configuration edits are ignored.** Because the baseline configuration is pinned, a candidate's edits to `tsconfig.json`, `package.json` and similar files are silently ignored by build and `typecheck_clean`, without a report.\n - **Other.** `node --check` exits 0 for a `.js` file written with ESM syntax even when it contains a syntax error (for example `export const a = ;`); a dangling symlink at an overlaid path fails closed with `EEXIST`, and so does a candidate symlink with a source extension; the pinned workspace is created under the system temporary directory, not the configured workspace root; `spec.candidate.cwd` is silently overridden by the pinned root; `--pretty` compiler output defeats the TypeScript diagnostic pattern (fails closed); legacy text-mode tool calls are not counted by the `toolCallSyntax` telemetry; the evaluator README is partly stale.\n \n+### Single-step mode and the TUI (WP-1106)\n+\n+These come from Entire's automated review of PR #402. They were not reproduced one by one by the release owner; the `--step` pause fix above was.\n+\n+- **The TUI `/step` command calls the turn runner directly.** It creates no rollback checkpoint, so a later `/undo` can discard the preceding turn's work along with the step; it emits no live tool events and saves no session; and a blocked turn is reported as \"Step completed\".\n+- **`/step` cannot be cancelled** from the TUI while its cycle runs (the command is not marked cancellable).\n+- **A stagnation stop is overwritten by the step pause**, and the pause skips the end-of-step bookkeeping, so stagnation detection does not advance across `/step` calls.\n+- **Test coverage is thin.** The REPL `/step` test does not drive the real command, and the TUI `/step` handler has no test.\n+\n+### Evaluation CLI and datasets (WP-1103, WP-1104, WP-1105)\n+\n+- **`--isolated-home` is honoured only when `eval` is the first argument.** With a global option in front (for example `nexus -e URL eval --dataset d --isolated-home`) the flag is accepted and ignored, and the run uses the operator's real home and configuration. Put `eval` first.\n+- **`diff_boundary` can reject a valid patch** for a non-hardened task that has hidden tests: the runner stages them into the candidate workspace before the comparison, so they count as agent changes.\n+- **Two sources of truth.** `eval/datasets/core-v1/tasks/*` copies `eval/tasks/*`, and the evaluator runtime file list exists in two places that already differ.\n+\n+### Other\n+\n+- **Tracing:** the OTLP instrumentation scope version is hard-coded to `1.1.0`, so trace records carry the wrong instrumentation version.\n+- **Tool grammar:** the GBNF compiler (`generateToolArgumentsGbnf`, `generateRegisteredToolArgumentGrammars`) has no runtime caller; only the tool allow-list is used. It ships with its own tests only.\n+- **Redaction layers:** sidecar text is redacted per field and again per event, which corrupted workspace paths badly enough to need an exception for `session.state`; file paths and diffs in other events are redacted twice with no such exception. Four identical diagnostic-redaction helpers exist in the CLI, and the REPL step error path skips redaction. Null stripping for optional tool arguments is also done twice.\n+\n ## Runtime requirements\n \n Unchanged from v1.1.1: Linux x86-64 with glibc 2.35 or newer, Node.js 22 or newer, and an OpenAI-compatible inference endpoint (local or an explicitly selected provider). The archive does not include Node.js. Rust and Cargo are not required at runtime.\n## v1/v112-release...origin/v1/v112-release [ahead 2]\n M docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md\n M docs/releases/v1.1.2.md\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1a83e-2c55-7f73-bb41-4b9fa47da4c8","create_time":1793824211.7524264}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
